Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs Markdown: complementary
	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-20
		Description: Markdown formats prose; STXT structures documents and adopts Markdown for the prose of its nodes. Two examples, and why MARKDOWN is the only embedded format in STXT schemas.

	Header: @STXT@ vs Markdown

	Assert >>
		Markdown gives format to prose, and @STXT@ gives structure to documents.
		They solve different problems, and @STXT@ adopts Markdown for the prose of its documents.

	Subheader: The data of a Markdown document

	Content >>
		Markdown has no fields. When a document needs data (author, date, status),
		the convention is **front matter**: a block between `---` lines, written in
		[YAML](stxt-vs-yaml), a second language. Validating that data takes a third:
		JSON Schema, or custom code.

	Listing >>
		---
		title: Style guide
		author: Ana López
		published: 2026-09-01
		---

		# Style guide

		Write **short** sentences. Prefer [links](https://example.com) to footnotes.

	Content >>
		In @STXT@ the data are nodes, and the prose is a block node:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.article): Style guide
			Author: Ana López
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Body >>
				Write **short** sentences. Prefer [links](https://example.com) to footnotes.

	Content >>
		And the template for its namespace:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.article
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.article):
					Author: (+)
					Published: (1) DATE
					Body: (1) MARKDOWN

	Content >>
		The validator checks what the front matter only suggested: there is an author, the date is a date
		and there is a body.

		`MARKDOWN` is the only embedded format that @STXT@ schemas define
		([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §9.7](stxt-schema-ref#s9-7)). It says that the text of the node is formatted prose,
		to be interpreted as CommonMark. @STXT@ does not interpret it, it hands it over as is.

	Subheader: Structure by heading convention

	Content >>
		When a Markdown document has fixed parts, the parts are headings. The agreement on
		which they are and what they contain lives outside the file. An architecture decision record (ADR):

	Listing >>
		# ADR 12: Adopt PostgreSQL

		## Status

		Accepted

		## Context

		The current store has no transactions across collections, and the
		billing module needs them.

		## Decision

		Migrate the billing data to PostgreSQL 16 during Q4.

	Content >>
		Whoever reads it knows that `Status` has four possible values, and that `Consequences` is missing here.
		No Markdown tool can check it, because to Markdown everything is headings and paragraphs.

		In @STXT@ the parts are nodes, and the prose of each one is still Markdown:

	Code >>
		ADR (com.example.adr): Adopt PostgreSQL
			Number: 12
			Status: Accepted
			Context >>
				The current store has no transactions across collections, and the
				billing module needs them.
			Decision >>
				Migrate the billing data to PostgreSQL 16 during Q4.
			Consequences >>
				The two services share one database until the migration ends.

	Content >>
		And the template that fixes the agreement:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.adr
			Structure >>
				ADR (com.example.adr):
					Number: (1) NATURAL
					Status: (1) ENUM [Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, Superseded]
					Context: (1) MARKDOWN
					Decision: (1) MARKDOWN
					Consequences: (1) MARKDOWN

	Content >>
		A document with errors does not validate:

	Code >>
		# ERROR: this document does not validate
		ADR (com.example.adr): Adopt PostgreSQL
			Number: 12
			# `Approved` is not a status of the list
			Status: Approved
			Context >>
				The current store has no transactions across collections.
			Decision >>
				Migrate the billing data to PostgreSQL 16 during Q4.
			# `Consequences` is missing

	Subheader: This site

	Content >>
		Every page of `stxt.dev`, this one included, is an @STXT@ document with its prose in Markdown.
		It is validated against the site template before being generated ([CMS and publishing](use-cases-cms)).
		The source of any page can be seen by adding `.stxt` to its address,
		for example [stxt-vs-markdown.stxt](stxt-vs-markdown.stxt).

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		* **Markdown alone**: a README, a comment or a gist. Lightly formatted prose with no validation.
		* **@STXT@**: when the document has named parts that must be checked. For example,
		  [CMS content](use-cases-cms), [corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs)
		  or [technical proposals](use-cases-rfcs). The prose inside is still Markdown.

		The short version of this page is in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-markdown).
